  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA slow moving, broad upper level trough is spinning over the \nNortheast today. The low and mid level flow is out of the \nnorthwest, and combined with 850mb temperatures of around -6C it\nis cold enough for lake effect showers. The showers will be \nmainly in the form of light snow early this morning, although \nsome rain, drizzle and even patchy freezing drizzle could mix in\nat times. A stronger shortwave disturbance will drop south into\nand across the area this afternoon and evening. This will \ninitiate widespread snow and rain showers. Boundary layer \ntemperatures will range mainly in the mid-30s to lower 40s this \nafternoon, as 850mb temperatures hover near -5C. These \nrelatively mild temperatures should prevent much in the way of \nany snow accumulation during the daytime hours today; even with \npersistent showers around. After sunset into this evening and \novernight there could be some minor snow accumulations (< 1\") \nmainly over the higher elevations, as the northwest flow keep \nlake effect/enhanced snow showers going. Overnight lows only dip\ndown into the upper 20s to lower 30s, so will again be \nmarginally cold enough for any accumulations. The cool northwest\nflow and lake effect snow/rain showers persist right through \nthe day on Sunday. There will be little change to atmospheric \nparameters and temperatures for this day. Increased PoPs above \nthe ensemble blend, as it appears quite likely scatter showers \nand overcast conditions will be around yet again.\n\nThe lake effect showers should gradually taper off to sprinkles and \nflurries heading into Sunday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe 850mb ridge axis does not pass through our area until Monday \nnight. Therefore, we remain locked in the cool, cloudy northwest \nflow pattern on Monday. Wouldn't be surprised to see pockets of \ndrizzle lingering into Monday under the thick stratus cloud deck. \nTemperatures remain cool only in the 40s on Monday.\n\nFor Tuesday through Saturday: General upper level ridging remains \nover the eastern US. There could be a very weak disturbance or front \npassing through toward Friday. Otherwise, high pressure looks to \nremain in control right through Saturday (if current timing holds). \nTemperatures will see a gradual warming trend through the week, \nreaching about 15 degrees above average for daytime highs by Friday \nand Saturday. With the generally clear skies and light winds, \novernight lows will be on the seasonably cool side, mainly in the \nupper 20s to 30s through the week. Overall a very pleasant and fair \nstretch of late fall weather looks to be in store.",
